"""Student guide to free AI tiers, verified against official plan pages."""
BODY = '''<section class="page-hero task-hero"><p class="eyebrow">FREE AI TOOLS / FOR STUDENTS</p><h1>Free AI tools for students: start with three, check the limits</h1><p class="lede">Start with one general assistant on a free plan, claim the Google student year if you are eligible, and keep a second assistant as backup. Check the official plan page before exam week: free tiers change, and this guide dates every claim.</p></section><article class="prose">
<h2>The short answer</h2><p>For most coursework, three free options cover the work: ChatGPT on its Free plan, Claude on its Free plan, and Google&rsquo;s one-year student offer (AI Pro in the US, AI Plus elsewhere). Pick one as your daily assistant. Use the second when the first hits a limit. Claim the Google student year only after reading the eligibility and renewal terms on the claim page.</p><p>Do not start by paying. Pay only when a free tier blocks real coursework more than once in a week, and only after you have checked what the paid tier adds on the official pricing page.</p>
<h2>Three verified free options</h2><p>Each entry below states what the provider&rsquo;s own page showed on 2026-10-14. Limits and prices change; confirm on the linked page before you rely on them.</p>
<h3>1. ChatGPT Free</h3><p>OpenAI&rsquo;s pricing page lists a Free plan with unlimited text chats, subject to abuse guardrails, plus limited messages with uploads, limited image creation, limited voice chats, limited deep research, limited memory and context, limited Codex access, and limited ChatGPT Work access on desktop. <a href="https://chatgpt.com/pricing" rel="noopener">Official pricing page</a>.</p><p>Use it for: drafting, explaining a concept, outlining an essay, brainstorming. Expect the tighter limits on uploads, images and research features, not on plain text chat.</p>
<h3>2. Claude Free</h3><p>Anthropic&rsquo;s pricing page lists a Free plan at $0: chat on web, desktop and mobile, web search, file creation, code execution, memory across conversations, app connectors, Artifacts, Claude Design, Slides and Docs, and up to 5 projects. Usage limits reset on a rolling five-hour session window, and Anthropic states there is no fixed message count: how much you can do depends on conversation length, model choice and features used. Paid Pro is listed at $17 per month with an annual subscription ($200 billed up front) or $20 billed monthly, with at least 5x more usage per five-hour session than Free. <a href="https://claude.com/pricing" rel="noopener">Official pricing page</a>.</p><p>Use it for: longer reasoning tasks, checking a draft against sources, working with files, building a small study aid. When you hit the limit, wait for the window to reset or switch to your backup assistant.</p>
<h3>3. Google student year</h3><p>Google&rsquo;s official announcement offers eligible college students one year of a Google AI plan free of charge. In the US, eligible students get one year of Google AI Pro free, described as 4x higher usage limits in Gemini, Gemini Spark, Gemini in Google apps such as Gmail and Google Docs, 5 TB of storage and Google Health Premium. Outside the US, eligible students get one year of Google AI Plus free, described as Gemini Omni, 2x higher usage limits and 400 GB of storage. <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/products/gemini-app/student-offer-google-ai/" rel="noopener">Official announcement</a>.</p><p>Before claiming, read the claim page for current eligibility, verification steps, the redemption window and what the plan renews to after the free year. Set a calendar reminder for the renewal date before you claim.</p>
<h2>Match one tool to each student job</h2><p>Do not collect tools. Assign each job to one assistant and learn its limits on real work.</p><ul><li><strong>Drafting and outlining:</strong> one general assistant. Ask for an outline with a source beside each factual claim, then write the draft yourself.</li><li><strong>Research:</strong> ask for sources first, answers second. Open every cited page and <a href="/ai-tools/check-ai-citations/">check cited evidence before using an AI answer</a>. A citation that does not support the claim is not evidence.</li><li><strong>Studying:</strong> turn your own notes into flashcards and practice quizzes. Test yourself from the cards, not from the chat transcript.</li><li><strong>Coding coursework:</strong> ask for an explanation of the error and a minimal fix, then run the tests yourself. Never paste secrets, credentials or other students&rsquo; work.</li><li><strong>Slides and documents:</strong> draft the content first, then format. Check every number in the deck against its source before presenting.</li></ul>
<h2>Five checks before you rely on any &ldquo;free&rdquo; tool</h2><ol><li><strong>Free plan or free trial?</strong> A free plan stays free with limits. A trial ends and may start charging. Read the plan page, not the advertisement.</li><li><strong>What happens at the limit?</strong> Find the reset rule (for example, a rolling window) and what the tool does when you reach it: pause, slow down, or ask you to upgrade.</li><li><strong>What does it renew to?</strong> Student offers and trials convert to paid plans. Read the renewal price and date before you claim, and set a reminder.</li><li><strong>What are you uploading?</strong> Do not upload ID documents, unpublished research, exam materials you did not write, or other people&rsquo;s private data. Once uploaded, you cannot fully control where it goes.</li><li><strong>What does your course allow?</strong> Your school and course policy governs AI use on assessed work. Disclose AI use when required. An AI answer is a draft until you verify it.</li></ol>
<h2>A one-week trial plan</h2><p><strong>Days 1&ndash;2:</strong> pick one general assistant and complete two real tasks with it: one draft and one explanation. <strong>Days 3&ndash;4:</strong> run one research task end to end, opening every cited source. <strong>Day 5:</strong> deliberately test a limit: upload a file, request images, and note what stops first. <strong>Day 6:</strong> if you are eligible, claim the Google student year and read the renewal terms. <strong>Day 7:</strong> decide. If the free tier carried the week, stay free. If it blocked real work twice, compare paid tiers on the official pages before spending.</p><p>If you hit a limit mid-task, <a href="/ai-tools/save-free-ai-progress/">save your progress before access stops</a> and resume with your backup assistant or after the reset window.</p>
<h2>Questions students ask</h2><details><summary>Do I need to pay for AI as a student?</summary><p>No, not to start. The three options above cover drafting, research, studying and coding help at $0. Pay only when a free tier repeatedly blocks real coursework, and only after comparing paid tiers on the official pricing pages.</p></details><details><summary>Can I use AI on assignments?</summary><p>Follow your course and school policy. Some courses allow AI for brainstorming and outlining but not for final text; some require disclosure. When in doubt, ask the instructor before submitting, and verify every factual claim in the output.</p></details><details><summary>What if the free tier changes?</summary><p>It will. Free tiers are revised often. Re-check the official plan page linked above before exam week, and treat any guide&rsquo;s figures as a dated snapshot, not a promise.</p></details><details><summary>Should I use several AI tools at once?</summary><p>Two is enough: a daily assistant and a backup. Each new tool adds another account, another set of limits and another place your uploads live. Add a third only for a job the first two cannot do.</p></details>
</article><section class="prose"><h2>Sources and review scope</h2><p>Plan details checked against the official pages linked above on 2026-10-14: OpenAI&rsquo;s ChatGPT pricing page, Anthropic&rsquo;s Claude pricing page, and Google&rsquo;s student-offer announcement. No prices or limits in this guide come from third-party roundups. This guide contains no affiliate links. For a general beginner workflow, use the <a href="/start/">beginner workflow</a> and the <a href="/free-ai/">free AI tools overview</a>.</p></section>'''
def publish_student_guide(page, cfg):
    page('/ai-tools/free-ai-tools-for-students/', 'Free AI tools for students: start with three, check the limits | '+cfg['brand'], 'Three verified free options for students: ChatGPT Free, Claude Free and Google\u2019s one-year student offer, with the checks to run before exam week.', BODY, lastmod='2026-10-14')
